140
00:10:42,926 --> 00:10:44,327
Maybe some of you are familiar with

141
00:10:45,103 --> 00:10:50,847
speculative lab buildings, but we thought it would be a good primer to hit on why we do some of the things that we do.

142
00:10:51,928 --> 00:10:54,570
So lab buildings on the left, office buildings on the right.

143
00:10:54,930 --> 00:11:01,495
Really what this gets down to is we've always heard that thinner buildings are better because it allows more light into the space.

144
00:11:01,935 --> 00:11:06,659
Well, that's true for office buildings where you have cubicles that may stretch from the window to the core.

145
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:12,463
But in a lab building, there's a quite a bit of support space that goes along with it.

146
00:11:13,632 --> 00:11:18,755
space that doesn't fit within the building core, space that doesn't need light and oftentimes doesn't want light.

147
00:11:20,076 --> 00:11:27,601
So what we see in lab buildings is they usually have a deeper, what we call lease span from the core to the window wall.

148
00:11:28,562 --> 00:11:36,007
And typically a good office or a good lab building starts at kind of that Goldilocks zone right in the middle of about 120 feet.

149
00:11:37,469 --> 00:11:39,370
What we've noticed over the past

150
00:11:39,890 --> 00:11:47,815
six months to a year, as there's been more of a push towards carbon reduction and more efficient buildings, all electric buildings.

151
00:11:49,156 --> 00:11:57,002
In order to achieve those goals, a lot more equipment, to be quite honestly frank with you, needs to go into the penthouse.

152
00:11:57,502 --> 00:12:00,684
And what we've noticed is at about 127 foot mark in the width of the building,

153
00:12:03,992 --> 00:12:17,257
we really struggle to fit a lot of that mechanical equipment up in the penthouse in order to overcome the larger electrical needs or process loads of a lab office building.

154
00:12:17,497 --> 00:12:25,400
So we'll get to why that's important in a little bit, but that 120 to 130 foot range is kind of the sweet spot.

155
00:12:25,780 --> 00:12:30,481
We can go larger, we have gone larger, but those become unique buildings and more purpose-built.

156
00:12:31,202 --> 00:12:31,942
Next slide, please.

157
00:12:34,259 --> 00:12:41,385
The other thing that's important about the grid is the cadence of the grid or the spacing of the structural grid.

158
00:12:41,405 --> 00:12:45,128
And those are usually in 11 foot modules, anywhere from 22 feet to 33 feet.

159
00:12:46,028 --> 00:12:50,952
A little bit different than an office building, but really this is predicated on lab equipment.

160
00:12:51,052 --> 00:12:53,354
It comes in a certain size, it's really not modifiable.

161
00:12:53,875 --> 00:12:58,238
And it comes down to a 30 inch lab bench, a five foot walking space and a 30 inch lab bench.

162
00:12:58,438 --> 00:13:00,340
And then you multiply that by two, you get 11 feet.

163
00:13:02,207 --> 00:13:16,413
The reason that this is important is when you're looking at typical office buildings compared to a lab office building, there may be a proportional difference that some of you might see, and it makes the building maybe look a little squatter because of this grid.

164
00:13:17,213 --> 00:13:18,153
Go to the next slide, please.

165
00:13:20,054 --> 00:13:21,535
The other difference is the floor-to-floor height.

166
00:13:22,795 --> 00:13:28,878
We can design great office buildings in 13 feet, sometimes even 12 foot 6, but with the amount of air

167
00:13:30,842 --> 00:13:37,026
and other equipment that we have in lab buildings in the interstitial space, 15 feet is really the gold standard.

168
00:13:38,286 --> 00:13:46,691
What we're trying to do here is not only working with RISE, but also the real big foray into lab and Medford is really starting out on the right foot.

169
00:13:47,451 --> 00:13:49,412
And that's getting to a 15 foot floor to floor.

170
00:13:49,992 --> 00:13:50,733
Next slide, please.

171
00:13:52,534 --> 00:13:53,854
The other big difference is a penthouse.

172
00:13:55,468 --> 00:14:02,652
we do a lot to disguise this and you'll see the team has come up with some good ways of disguising the penthouse and what we're going to show you.

173
00:14:02,772 --> 00:14:06,074
But this really ties also ties back to the width of the building.

174
00:14:06,575 --> 00:14:11,398
As that building gets narrower, there's a set amount of equipment that needs to go up in this roof.

175
00:14:11,458 --> 00:14:16,461
And it's kind of like one of those toys if you have kids where you squish it in one direction and it grows in the other.

176
00:14:16,561 --> 00:14:19,422
So the narrower a building gets, the taller this gets.

177
00:14:19,483 --> 00:14:23,925
So we'd like to keep it around 40, 45 feet, but we have some buildings on the narrower side.

178
00:14:23,965 --> 00:14:24,606
This is close to 53.

179
00:14:26,187 --> 00:14:31,089
There are a lot of things that we can do to hide it, and you'll see some of the things that the team is proposing.

180
00:14:32,190 --> 00:14:32,870
Next slide, please.

181
00:14:35,891 --> 00:14:36,371
